{{nihongo|'''Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto'''|天社土御門神道}}<ref>https://archive.ph/uJNu5</ref> is a form of [[Confucian Shinto]]. <ref>{{Cite web |title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細 |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/id=8943 |access-date=2023-03-10 |website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム |language=ja}}</ref>It is also known as [[Tsuchimikado Shinto]] 

It was founded by [[Abe no Seimei]] and influenced by [[Onmyodo]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細 |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9907 |access-date=2023-03-06 |website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム |language=ja}}</ref> in the 900s<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Cali |first1=Joseph |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=J1gEEAAAQBAJ&dq=tsuchimikado+shinto&pg=PA137 |title=Shinto Shrines: A Guide to the Sacred Sites of Japan's Ancient Religion |last2=Dougill |first2=John |date=2012-11-30 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-3775-4 |language=en}}</ref> It is highly linked to the [[Abe clan]].<ref name=":0" />

The current head is Yoshihito Fujita<ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-10-05 |title=天社土御門神道本庁 |url=https://japanese-religions.com/jr4210005008880/ |access-date=2023-03-06 |website=JAPANESE RELIGIONS |language=ja}}</ref>  

Tsuchimikado Shintō was abolished in [[1870]] with the ban of [[Onmyōdō]], and only permitted again in [[1945]] with religious freedom and the abolition of [[State Shinto]].<ref name=":0" /> A new organization was created for it in [[1954]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=備忘録 天社土御門神道 本庁 |url=http://kashomemorandum.blog.fc2.com/blog-entry-137.html |access-date=2023-03-06 |website=kashomemorandum.blog.fc2.com}}</ref> 

