It was founded by [[Abe no Seimei|Abe no Seimei]] and influenced by [[Onmyodo]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細 |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9907 |access-date=2023-03-06 |website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム |language=ja}}</ref> in the 900s<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Cali |first1=Joseph |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=J1gEEAAAQBAJ&dq=tsuchimikado+shinto&pg=PA137 |title=Shinto Shrines: A Guide to the Sacred Sites of Japan's Ancient Religion |last2=Dougill |first2=John |date=2012-11-30 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-3775-4 |language=en}}</ref> It is highly linked to the [[Abe clan]].<ref name=":0" />

It is considered the modern form of Onmyodo.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Makoto |first=Hayashi |last2=Luers |first2=Dylan |last3=林淳 |date=2013 |title=The Development of Early Modern Onmyōdō |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/41955534 |journal=Japanese Journal of Religious Studies |volume=40 |issue=1 |pages=151–167 |issn=0304-1042}}</ref>

The current head is Yoshihito Fujita<ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-10-05 |title=天社土御門神道本庁 |url=https://japanese-religions.com/jr4210005008880/ |access-date=2023-03-06 |website=JAPANESE RELIGIONS |language=ja}}</ref>

It was banned by the {{interlanguage link|Tensha Shinto Prohibition Ordinance|ja|天社禁止令}}  

Tsuchimikado Shintō was abolished in [[1870|1870]] with the ban of [[Onmyōdō|Onmyōdō]], and only permitted again in [[1945]] with religious freedom and the abolition of [[State Shinto]].<ref name=":0" /> A new organization was created for it in [[1954]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=備忘録 天社土御門神道 本庁 |url=http://kashomemorandum.blog.fc2.com/blog-entry-137.html |access-date=2023-03-06 |website=kashomemorandum.blog.fc2.com}}</ref>

