{{afc comment|1=This was a bad abridged translation earlier, merged in the original enwiki article too[[User:Immanuelle|Immanuelle]] ([[User talk:Immanuelle|talk]]) 07:49, 27 March 2025 (UTC)}} {{Cite source|date=October 8, 2019 (Tuesday)}} {{Infobox Organization | Name = Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto Honcho | image = | image size = | Caption = | Established = {{ill|1954|ja|昭和29th year }} (January 11) | Type = {{ill|Religious Corporation|ja|宗教法人}} | Status = {{ill|Incorporated religious corporation under the jurisdiction of the Minister of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology|ja|文部科学大臣所轄包括宗教法人}} | Headquarters = 129-9, Nadajō, Nata-shō, Ōi Town, Ōi District, Fukui Prefecture | Founder = {{ill|Tsuchimikado Noriitada|ja|土御門範忠}} | President = Fujita Yuji (Honcho Leader) | People = {{ill|Tsuchimikado Noriitada|ja|土御門範忠}}, {{ill|Fujita Kando|ja|藤田乾堂}} | Website = https://onmyodo.jp | Remarks = }}
'''Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto''' (天社土御門神道, Tensha Tsuchimikado Shintō), or simply '''Tsuchimikado Shinto''' (土御門神道, Tsuchimikado Shintō), is a [[religious organization]] headquartered in [[Ōi Town]], [[Ōi District]], [[Fukui Prefecture]] (formerly Nata-shō Village). The name "Tsuchimikado Shinto" is generally used, but it is also referred to as "Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto," incorporating the term "Tensha" from "Tensha Shrine" (天社宮), a shrine name bestowed by [[Reigen Emperor]] upon the Tsuchimikado family during the Edo period as the "Soke of Onmyōdō."
Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto is rooted in the [[Onmyōdō]] (yin-yang way) traditions passed down by the {{ill|Abe clan|ja|安倍氏}}'s {{ill|Tsuchimikado family|ja|土御門家}}, descendants of [[Abe no Seimei]]. However, it evolved into "Tensha Shinto" by incorporating influences from [[Yoshida Shinto]] in the medieval period and [[Suika Shinto]] proposed by the [[Edo period]] Shintoist and Confucian scholar {{ill|Yamazaki Ansai|ja|山崎闇斎}}. Strictly speaking, it is no longer purely Onmyōdō but is considered a Shinto-based organization{{efn2|While it is classified under "Miscellaneous Religions" in the religious corporation registry and not under {{ill|Sect Shinto|ja|教派神道}} or {{ill|Shrine Shinto|ja|神社神道}}.<ref name="nisino">[[#nisino|Nishino Shrine Journal]]</ref>}}. Currently, its leadership does not reside with the head of the Tsuchimikado family but rather with the Fujita family, which historically managed the household affairs of the Tsuchimikado family.
After the last Tsuchimikado family head, {{ill|Tsuchimikado Noriitada|ja|土御門範忠}} (1920–1994), passed away, the position of "Chief Priest" was abolished. Media and public relations are handled under the title "Leader of Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto Honcho" (庁長) or simply "Representative." The current highest-ranking official (representative of the organization) holds the title "Honcho Leader."
