== Historical Background == The origins of Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto trace back to the [[Abe clan|Abe family]]'s establishment of Onmyōdō as a hereditary discipline focusing on {{ill|Astronomy|ja|天文道|1=Tenmondō (astrology)|lt=Tenmondō|qq= (astrology)|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Astronomy|13=User:Immanuelle/Tenmondō (astrology)|qq=}}, {{ill|Calendar Science|ja|暦道|1=Rekidō|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Calendar Science|13=User:Immanuelle/Rekidō|qq=}}, and {{ill|Onmyōdō|ja|陰陽道|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Onmyōdō|qq=}} during the [[Muromachi period]]. The family name "Tsuchimikado" was adopted after {{ill|Abe no Ariyo|ja|安倍有世|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Abe no Ariyo|qq=}} was granted the extraordinary court Rank of Junior Second Rank by {{ill|Ashikaga Yoshimitsu|ja|足利義満|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ashikaga Yoshimitsu|qq=}}. During the [[Ōnin War]], the family evacuated their estates in Kyoto and relocated to the Nata-shō area in {{ill|Wakasa Province|ja|若狭国|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Wakasa Province|qq=}}, where they were su11orted by local warlords such as the {{ill|Wakasa Takeda clan|ja|若狭武田氏|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Wakasa Takeda clan|qq=}}.
Despite this relocation, much of the Onmyōdō knowledge and practices maintained by the {{ill|Onmyōryō|ja|陰陽寮|1=Yin-Yang Bureau|2=ja|3=陰陽寮|4=fr|5=Bureau d'Onmyō|6=zh|7=阴阳寮|8=ko|9=음양료|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Onmyōryō|13=User:Immanuelle/Yin-Yang Bureau|qq=}} were lost during the [[Ōnin War]]. Efforts were made to recover elements of Onmyōdō by incorporating practices from {{ill|Yoshida Shinto|ja|吉田神道|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yoshida Shinto|qq=}} and elements influenced by esoteric Buddhism.
== Development into Tensha Shinto == Through the leadership of {{ill|Tsuchimikado Taifuku|ja|土御門泰福|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tsuchimikado Taifuku|qq=}}, Tensha Shinto emerged as a synthesis of Onmyōdō, Yoshida Shinto practices, and Suika Shinto's Confucian-based teachings. Taifuku was a disciple of {{ill|Yamazaki Ansai|ja|山崎闇斎|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yamazaki Ansai|qq=}}, who emphasized reverence for the imperial lineage and divine sovereignty.
== Post-Meiji Restoration and Revival == With the [[Meiji Restoration]], Onmyōdō and Tensha Shinto were banned under the {{ill|Decree Prohibiting Tensha|ja|天社禁止令|1=Tensha Shinto Prohibition Ordinance|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Decree Prohibiting Tensha|13=User:Immanuelle/Tensha Shinto Prohibition Ordinance|qq=}} in 1870. The Tsuchimikado family ceased to engage in Onmyōdō practices, and the Tensha Shinto organization faced dissolution.
The revival of Tensha Shinto began in {{ill|Shōwa period|ja|昭和|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shōwa period|qq=}} 29 (1954) with its incorporation as a religious corporation. Its headquarters was established in Fukui Prefecture, and the Fujita family assumed leadership roles.
== Current Status == Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto currently operates under the leadership of Fujita Yoshihito. The organization oversees several affiliated shrines and religious institutions. Efforts have been made to promote Tensha Shinto as part of local cultural heritage, su11orted by collaborations between academia, local governments, and private enterprises.
