{{Unreferenced|date=October 8, 2019 (Tuesday)}} {{Infobox Organization | Name = Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto Honcho | image = | image size = | Caption = | Established = {{ill|1954|ja|昭和29年|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/1954|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 1954年}} (January 11) | Type = {{ill|Religious Corporation|ja|宗教法人|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Religious Corporation|qq=}} | Status = {{ill|Incorporated religious corporation under the jurisdiction of the Minister of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology|ja|文部科学大臣所轄包括宗教法人|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Incorporated religious corporation under the jurisdiction of the Minister of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} | Headquarters = 129-9, Nadajō, Nata-shō, Ōi Town, Ōi District, Fukui Prefecture | Founder = {{ill|Tsuchimikado Noriitada|ja|土御門範忠|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tsuchimikado Noriitada|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} | President = Fujita Yuji (Honcho Leader) | People = {{ill|Tsuchimikado Noriitada|ja|土御門範忠|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tsuchimikado Noriitada|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}, {{ill|Fujita Kando|ja|藤田乾堂|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujita Kando|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} | Website = https://onmyodo.jp | Remarks = }}
'''Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto''' (天社土御門神道, Tensha Tsuchimikado Shintō), or simply '''Tsuchimikado Shinto''' (土御門神道, Tsuchimikado Shintō), is a [[religious organization]] headquartered in [[Ōi Town]], [[Ōi District]], [[:en:Fukui Prefecture|Fukui Prefecture]] (formerly Nata-shō Village). The name "Tsuchimikado Shinto" is generally used, but it is also referred to as "Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto," incorporating the term "Tensha" from "Tensha Shrine" (天社宮), a shrine name bestowed by [[Reigen Emperor]] upon the Tsuchimikado family during the Edo period as the "Soke of Onmyōdō."
Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto is rooted in the [[Onmyōdō]] (yin-yang way) traditions passed down by the {{ill|Abe clan|ja|安倍氏|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Abe clan|qq=}}'s {{ill|Tsuchimikado clan|lt=Tsuchimikado clan|lt=Tsuchimikado family|ja|土御門家|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tsuchimikado family|qq=}}, descendants of [[Abe no Seimei]]. However, it evolved into "Tensha Shinto" by incorporating influences from [[Yoshida Shinto]] in the medieval period and [[Suika Shinto]] proposed by the [[:en:Edo period|Edo period]] Shintoist and Confucian scholar [[:en:Yamazaki Ansai|Yamazaki Ansai]]. Strictly speaking, it is no longer purely Onmyōdō but is considered a Shinto-based organization{{efn2|While it is classified under "Miscellaneous Religions" in the religious corporation registry and not under [[:en:Sect Shinto|Sect Shinto]] or [[:en:Shrine Shinto|Shrine Shinto]].<ref name="nisino">[[#nisino|Nishino Shrine Journal]]</ref>}}. Currently, its leadership does not reside with the head of the Tsuchimikado family but rather with the Fujita family, which historically managed the household affairs of the Tsuchimikado family.
After the last Tsuchimikado family head, {{ill|Tsuchimikado Noriitada|ja|土御門範忠|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tsuchimikado Noriitada|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} (1920–1994), passed away, the position of "Chief Priest" was abolished. Media and public relations are handled under the title "Leader of Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto Honcho" (庁長) or simply "Representative." The current highest-Ranking official (representative of the organization) holds the title "Honcho Leader."
==Overview==
{{Short description|Shinto religious organization}} {{Use dmy dates|date=December 2023}}
{{Infobox organization | name = Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto | founder = [[Abe no Seimei]] | founding_location = Japan | website = https://onmyodo.jp }}
{{nihongo|'''Tensha Tsuchimikado Shinto'''|天社土御門神道}}<ref>https://archive.today/uJNu5</ref> is a form of [[Confucian Shinto]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細 |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/id=8943 |access-date=2023-03-10 |website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム |language=ja}}</ref> It is also known as Tsuchimikado Shinto
It was founded by [[Abe no Seimei]] and influenced by [[Onmyodo]].<ref name="國學院大學テシタルミューシアム-2023a">{{Cite web |title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細 |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9907 |access-date=2023-03-06 |website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム |language=ja}}</ref> in the 900s<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Cali |first1=Joseph |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=J1gEEAAAQBAJ&dq=tsuchimikado+shinto&pg=PA137 |title=Shinto Shrines: A Guide to the Sacred Sites of Japan's Ancient Religion |last2=Dougill |first2=John |date=30 November 2012 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-3775-4 |language=en}}</ref> It is highly linked to the [[Abe clan]].<ref name="國學院大學テシタルミューシアム-2023a" />
It is considered the modern form of Onmyodo.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Makoto |first=Hayashi |last2=Luers |first2=Dylan |last3=林淳 |date=2013 |title=The Development of Early Modern Onmyōdō |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/41955534 |journal=Japanese Journal of Religious Studies |volume=40 |issue=1 |pages=151–167 |issn=0304-1042}}</ref>
The current head is Yoshihito Fujita<ref>{{Cite web |date=5 October 2015 |title=天社土御門神道本庁 |url=https://japanese-religions.com/jr4210005008880/ |access-date=2023-03-06 |website=JAPANESE RELIGIONS |language=ja}}</ref>
It was banned by the {{interlanguage link|Tensha Shinto Prohibition Ordinance|ja|天社禁止令}}
Tsuchimikado Shintō was abolished in 1870 with the ban of [[Onmyōdō]], and only permitted again in 1945 with religious freedom and the abolition of [[State Shinto]].<ref name="國學院大學テシタルミューシアム-2023a" /> A new organization was created for it in 1954.<ref>{{Cite web |title=備忘録 天社土御門神道 本庁 |url=http://kashomemorandum.blog.fc2.com/blog-entry-137.html |access-date=2023-03-06 |website=kashomemorandum.blog.fc2.com}}</ref>
